# Manipuri (Meitei, Meeteilon) in the Meetei Mayek script (U+ABC0-U+ABFF and
# the extensions U+AAE0-U+AAFF). Text in Bengali script is first written in
# Meetei Mayek by src/mni.rs (`bengali_to_meetei`) and read by this file too.
# espeak-ng 1.52 has no Meitei voice (its `bpy` voice is Bishnupriya
# Manipuri, another language) and reads Meetei Mayek letters as their code
# points, so there is no reference reading to be compatible with: both modes
# give this file's reading. Written for uccharan from these descriptions:
#   Michael Everson, "Proposal for encoding the Meitei Mayek script in the
#     BMP of the UCS" (ISO/IEC JTC1/SC2/WG2 N3206R2, Unicode L2/07-005R2,
#     2007), and the Irish and US national bodies, "Proposed Encoding for
#     Meetei Mayek Block" (N3473, L2/08-232, 2008; the block ABC0-ABFF, the
#     extensions AAE0-AAFF): the letter values; a consonant letter carries
#     the inherent vowel; the lonsum letters ꯛ ꯜ ꯝ ꯞ ꯟ ꯠ ꯡ (and ꯢ, "now often
#     counted as a final Y") are explicit finals without a vowel; ꯭ (apun
#     iyek) kills a vowel, its use optional ("kara or kra"); ꯪ (nung) is
#     the anusvara; ꯬ (lum iyek) is a heavy-tone mark; modern spelling
#     sometimes tells words apart by tone (the proposal's kan "chariot"
#     against a falling-tone kan "mosquito");
#   Shobhana L. Chelliah (1997), A Grammar of Meithei (Mouton de Gruyter),
#     and D. N. S. Bhat and M. S. Ningomba (1997), Manipuri Grammar (LINCOM),
#     as summarised in the Wikipedia article "Meitei language": the
#     phonemes (vowels i e ɐ a o u; p t tʃ k, pʰ tʰ kʰ, b d dʒ ɡ, bʱ dʱ ɡʱ
#     in loanwords, m n ŋ, s h, ɾ, l, w j); the central vowel, written ə in
#     much recent work, is said [ɐ], and before a glide it is [e] or [o]
#     (/ɐj/ [ej], /ɐw/ [ow], written ei and ou here); Meitei has two (some
#     say three) lexical tones, which neither script writes: they are not
#     read here;
#   Wiktionary, Manipuri entries with a pronunciation (CC BY-SA 4.0), for
#     the treatment of a final letter without a vowel sign.
# Tone is read from neither the spelling nor ꯬: words are read by their
# letters (ꯥ is a).
# Choices made here where the descriptions leave it open, checked on the
# development half of the gold set and on IndicCorp v2 text (CC0): a final
# consonant letter keeps its inherent vowel after ꯕ ꯅ ꯄ ꯗ ꯒ ꯃ ꯇ ꯀ (the
# consonants of the suffixes -bɐ -nɐ -pɐ -dɐ -ɡɐ -mɐ -tɐ -kɐ: ꯑꯁꯤꯗ ɐsidɐ,
# ꯂꯩꯕ lɐibɐ) and drops it after the others, which end loanwords (ꯃꯅꯤꯄꯨꯔ
# mɐnipuɾ, ꯁꯍꯔ sɐhɐɾ); ꯏ ꯎ ꯌ after a vowel sign and before a consonant or the
# end of the word close a diphthong (ꯂꯥꯏ lai, ꯆꯥꯎ tʃau, ꯃꯈꯣꯌ mɐkʰoi); ꯏ ꯎ
# right after a consonant letter make ꯩ ꯧ with its vowel (ꯄꯨꯔꯀꯏ puɾɐkei).
# Closed syllables and suffix vowels that the spelling leaves to the reader
# (ꯃꯇꯃꯗꯥ mɐtɐmdɐ, ꯂꯥꯎꯕꯥ laubɐ) are written out by src/mni.rs (`prepare`)
# before this file reads the text. Meitei has no stress accent to mark:
# words carry no stress marks. The extension letters for Sanskrit and Pali
# sounds keep their Unicode values.

[meta]
tag = mni
block = abc0
stress = none
final-secondary = no
deletion = final
final-keep-after = b n p d ɡ m t k
inherent = ɐ ɐ
anusvara = velar

